{{> judge-opening}}

JUDGMENT POINT: THE ARTICLE'S LANGUAGE (kogaki#1417): `language` in the input is the one language the article is written in, declared by the Audience file; write every proposition you compose in it, and keep in English only the keys and ids the record's shape fixes. compose ONE LEG for this Brief -- the Leg that builds the RelationalStructure in the input, `structure`, for the reader, opening from what the Persona already knows, rendered in the input as `persona_prior_knowledge` (kogaki#1664, kogaki#1699). A Leg carries no reader state (kogaki#1699) and binds no Move (kogaki#1768): the paragraph Move is bound per Brief Waypoint by /paragraph-plan, after the Brief (kogaki#1375). The Harness marks the last Leg `reaches_target` (kogaki#1532). EVERY LEG CARRIES ITS WAYPOINTS (kogaki#1311): the ordered route through the Leg, ONE BRIEF WAYPOINT PER ENTRY OF `claims` IN THE INPUT, IN ORDER (kogaki#1664). {{> move-extraction-playground/each-waypoint-is-a-point}} Brief Waypoint N is what the reader must come to hold for Claim N. THE WORDS ARE THE STRUCTURE'S (kogaki#1664): this Brief composes no renderings record; Claim N's `proposition` and Brief Waypoint N's `point` are written in the words of entry N's `material` -- a word that is in none of it and is not a function word is refused naming the word. Write the points from the structure's Role `context` and relation `holds` sentences, IN ORDER (kogaki#1664). EACH WAYPOINT RECORDS ITS `case` (kogaki#1732): `own` where its paragraph tells the subject's own case, `apart` where it tells a case apart from the subject, in material the reader already holds -- a record of what you decided when you wrote the waypoint, chosen from the Persona's prior knowledge; it adds no paragraph, and the waypoint after an `apart` one carries that case's structure onto the subject, so a Leg never ends on `apart`. THE STRUCTURE STANDS IN FOR STRANDS (kogaki#1664): Claim N is the `holds` sentence of entry N of `claims` in the input, addressed `L<N>` as its `claim_strand`, and its `proposition` is that sentence word for word; the Leg carries ONE Claim per entry. Brief Waypoint N's `point` is written from entry N's `material`, the `context` of each Role and the `holds` of each relation the entry relates. The Leg carries exactly as many Brief Waypoints as `claims` has entries.
REQUIRED RECORD SHAPE: ONE record AT THE RECORD'S OWN ROOT carrying `legs`, an array of EXACTLY ONE Leg, and nothing else. The Leg carries exactly these keys: `leg_id`; `claims` -- one `{ "type": "strand", "strand": "L<N>", "proposition": <entry N's holds, word for word> }` per entry of `claims` in the input, in order; and `waypoints` -- one `{ "point": <one sentence>, "case": "own" | "apart" }` per entry, in order. Every field means what src/leg-schema.json, rendered below, says it means. A Leg binds no Move (kogaki#1768), and the Harness writes nothing onto it.
WHAT IS REFUSED: a key the record or the Leg does not declare, or a declared key missing; not exactly one Leg; a Claim whose `proposition` is not its entry's `holds`; and the Move-Extraction Playground's `validateLegPathUnit` over the Leg, imported, with each entry's `material` as the text its Claim and point are recovered from. Each refusal is put back into your next round verbatim.

